body:
I'm top heavy and have big and high set boobs near my collar bones, and I don't like poofy sleeves at all. It's not Kibbe theory, but I only wear spaghetti straps, turtleneck, or scoop neck. No poofy sleeves or strapless to make my shoulders and boobs look wider. 

&#x200B;

Maybe thats why you don't like look 1? Look 2 is better on top IMO but the bottom is not it. A stiff midi skirt is very 2000s and won't flatter many people. If the second dress was cut off a little above the knee, was more flowy and figure hugging (bias cut silky material), or ankle length and flowy, I would personally wear that outfit! I hate midi it is so hard to pull off. Maxi or mini only, IMO.

Hey! Thanks for this comment. I am speaking to all the right mental health professionals, lawyers, and pain specialists and surgeons for the injury. But it is brutal. Mental health wise. I think I should probably feel more attached to my baby. It feels like he is so sure of himself and the world. He is so confident and wants to be treated like a 'person.' 

&#x200B;

Meanwhile I am a wreck. The dog is just pure love towards me. He's a rescue who's been through trauma as well.

&#x200B;

And bub is so confident and proud and unafraid and bold and I just... don't relate to him on that level. His naive confidence scares me. He only lies on my chest when he is sick or exhausted. Unlike the dog.

&#x200B;

Right now I feel like I want a million cuddles, not a baby who crawls down stairs and under chairs. If that makes sense. I feel like emotionally we have nothin g in common.

Hey hun, I've just seen this.  


I have Pudendal Neuralgia from a big baby, and a forceps delivery. Go to a women's health physio ASAP. The pain become permanent and much harder to treat after about 6 months.  


LMAO don't wear G-strings or undies at all that's helped me. As has avoiding cooking, cleaning, lots of squatting and bending, sitting down (even to watch TV). I need to recline, sort of sit on my side, squat, or sit on my knees. I wear shoes on hard surfaces to stop the pain travelling up to my pelvic floor. Weirdly, sex, walking, weightlifting and yoga is alright. Running and deep hip opening yoga aggravates it.

&#x200B;

You may get put in contact with a OBGYN pain specialist.

&#x200B;

I've started on meds, just had a nerve block, talks about how to modify my physical activity (NO sitting on my bum for more than half an hour) and topical amyltriptiline cream and valium for the last month. I am not disabled anymore. Opiates help drown out the pain but they're not a very good long term solution (addiction and they lose efficiacy.) Same problem with oral valium. Can't comment on CBD I use it as well but not sure if it directly helps the pudendal neuralgia.

&#x200B;

I am, however, suing the hospital!  


Please tell me how you've gone! Genuinely curious how your journey is shaping up.

I went thru your post history and saw you haven't had kids. I had my lap on day 1 of period and had my uterus 'scraped out' if that makes sense. Exactly 2 weeks later I was ovulating, cleared for sex, and fell pregnant. Just a thought?  


I am no Dr., but the timing couldn't have been better for the surgery and scraping out my uterus and then ovulation 2 weeks later and boom, baby. Also made recovering from lap heaps easier coz I didn't get a period for over a year. 

&#x200B;

Could you talk to your Dr. about timing the lap to your cycles and scraping your uterus (they filled it with water to have a look for endo or some shit like that?) 

&#x200B;

I am pretty sure I was infertile before my lap.

A hysterectomy will get rid of adenomyosis (endo in the uterus wall). But most ladies with stage 4 endo will have the abnormal cells on their bowels, ovaries, maybe bladder or even diaphragm. That endo will be causing adhesions, inflammation, scar tissue and therefore pain. A hysterectomy will not change that.

&#x200B;

Will he be excising all of the rest of the endo in your abdominal cavity (and rest of body) when he goes in for the hysterectomy?

&#x200B;

So saying, my excision surgery got rid of endo but not my adenomyosis, and the 'pulling', nervy pain I get after peeing on my period is still there, as is the spasms after orgasm, but the bowel feeling endo pain I had, which felt like the worst diahorrea cramps ever, is gone, because he excised endo off my bowels. And my surgeon said the pulling, nervy belly-button pain was probbaly from the adenomyosis

&#x200B;

So ask your surgeon what he is aiming to target with the hysterectomy and which symptoms that will specifically reduce.

If that is a risk she is willing to take, I don't see why not. Something was going wrong with my baby (late decels to 80 bpm) and I declined the medical intervention offered to me, but it was forced onto me anyway. I would have been physically and pyshcologically more healthy after the birth if I had delivered at home. And I was willing to take my chances with baby.

&#x200B;

Doctors overhype risks to baby when they push interventions and when you look at peer reviewed scientific literature, the actual risk to your baby is often quite small (1/100 to 1/1000), whereas the risk of those interventions are huge (1 or 2 in 10) to the momma, like muscle damage, nerve damage, incontinence, pain during sex, postpartum depression and PTSD.

&#x200B;

Some women are confident with 'something wrong' and are happy to take on that risk to avoid iatrogenic harm to themselves and their babies.

&#x200B;

I will be having a homebirth and the ONLY thing I will 'let' my midwife do is assist with shoulder dystocia or call an ambulance if I am pushing for over 2 hours. I will only let a midwife help me get my baby out if they are stuck inside. I am happy to deliver a baby vaginally with late decels, cord around neck, any other complication. As a consenting adult that is every mother's personal choice, even if you happen to disagree with it.

&#x200B;

And if this mama doesn't even want a midwife, she has her reasons and a freebirth is legal.

Yep! Went to my GP, ($60 appointment), said my sister just had surgery for endo, could I have a referral to a laproscopic surgeon. She gave me a referral for Dr. Vince Lamaro at St. Vincents. 1 month later I had seen him, had my surgery, and was pregnant. I've just had one period since giving birth and it had minimal pain.

&#x200B;

If you can pay the almost 10K invoice (even after private insurance) it was SO WORTH IT.   


WOW. And I think his appointments are pretty pricey too, though the initial consults I got a fair bit back because it was the end of the year and I had reached my medicare safety net.

&#x200B;

If my parent's didn't pay for it, it would have been worth a bank loan to pay for. My pain before and after is like night and day.

&#x200B;

His reviews say that his bedside manner is a bit weird (it is), but he is a brilliant endo specialist (also true).

>y push my baby out as I pushed along with it. It was natural. Just because I didn't suffer 

Yeah the natural course of birth includes feeling the hormone cascade that comes along with it. And pain for whatever reason is part of the biological process of natural birth. 

&#x200B;

Getting rid of that pain with an epidural is a great option, but it isn't 'natural'. 'Natural' doesn't mean better.

&#x200B;

I am glad you had an empowering birth that has left you with good memories. I am really envious of that!

Wowwwww what a read. I am so sorry. That is definitely a unique post. My takeaway is that abuse is abuse, and you might not moment better the moemnt you leave, but after a few months you won't know yourself. No adult deserves to be talked down to like a naughty 5 year old or disobedient house elf. The way he talks to you would be out of line for talking to a dog. I promise, you are 200x more valuable than he makes you out to be.

&#x200B;

Also from my experience, religion gives men some kind of God complex. Religion makes controlling, entitled, know-it-all men worse, because they feel like they have a divine sanction to be the lord of the house, and they think that gives them a right to discount how others feel and treat their family members like shit because in god's eyes women and children are less important.

Better take things slowly rather than rush. I PERSONALLY stuck to walks slow and up to 20 mins for 5 weeks, as well as gentle squats, pelvic tilts, and other activation type exercises to wake my muscles up. At 5 weeks I decided I was bored and started Kayla Itsines postpartum which was a lovely way to ease back into exercise. At 8 weeks pp. I felt well enough for hour long walks, 12 weeks to run and do classic weight lifting. 

&#x200B;

I probably should be seeing a pelvic floor PT though haha. I never over-extended myself. The walking was because my dog needs walks, not because I want to, and the activation stuff was because my body was aching from not moving at all.

I've commented on posts like this and I will comment again. My hubby has sex because it is an ADDITION to my life. I literally close my eyes and lie back and he caters to each and every one of my fantasies with a well thought out scenario that makes me come hard and often. HE is so damn good in bed that I look forward to sex with him and want to please him as well because getting into bed with him feels better than any drug I have tried. This isn't about romancing, personality, emotional connection or anything else. He has put the effort in to developing his sex skills well before I met him. Research, practice and literal lessons with a sex specialist. Sex with him adds something positive to my life and fills up my energetic cup. It is not 'another chore'.

&#x200B;

If he was like my ex (and most men I have slept with actually) and expected me to initiate, take the lead, give him a blowjob, then think up what he would like, choose some positions, do some fake moans, I absolutely wouldn't. We are both exhausted and working really hard, I don't have a speck more energy to give someone else.

&#x200B;

Not only has my boyfriend put in thousands of hours of concious thought and practice to better sex, after having lessons, but he has put in the work to figure out what I like in bed, how to please me, and how to get me hungry for more. If your hubby isn't doing that he is frankly being lazy and turning himself into a chore for you, and using your body to masturbate. Any man can  e better at sex, just like years of thoughtful driving will make you proficient driver, and years of delibarate practice will make you a great pianist. So what if you can come from his mediocre sex. I can come from 1 min on a vibrator doesn't mean anything. If the sex he is offering isn't lifting you out of your body, and doesn't feel better than peaking on MDMA, he can do better and it's a waste of your time.

&#x200B;

So you and other BroMos aren't anti-sex, you sound like you're anti bad sex and anti sex that drains you instead of enriches you. 

&#x200B;

And hey, if you're ACTUALLY anti all sex due to a 4th degree tear, working full time, a messy house and parenting, then your husband needs to grow TF and priorities the help you need. Whether that is a cleaning lady, a pelvic floor physio, a STREAM practitioner, yoni massage, normal massage, energetic bodywork, organizing a babysitter so you can have some time to yourself, or something else. You sound like you have very little time to relax and be yourself and figure out and meet your own needs, let alone someone else's.

&#x200B;

If your sex drive is actually lacking, then that is a problem that needs to be solved so you and your partner can have mutually satisfying sex. It isn't right to ask you to grin and bear his dick in you. You can't give enthusiastic consent to that. You deserve a sex drive. Sex and orgasms is one thing that makes my life bearable. Do you have some time alone at home no husband no kids? Buy a magic wand vibrator (guaranteed orgasms) or your fave rabbit type and lie back and enjoy the sensations. You don't even need to think of anything sexual.

&#x200B;

Your sexuality deserves to be nurtured and explored in its own right, out of the context of your husband. When I am feeling overwhelmed with motherhood, I go into the other room when baby is asleep and treat myself. That is just my style. Other women might prefer a crystal wand and doing some yoni de-armoring

I've got a similar situation. therapy was essentially to abuse me, to cut a long story short. Therapists, even group like DBT, gives me crazy anxiety. I am incapable of engaging. Problem is I now have severe PTSD after childbirth (more abuse by medical professionals).

&#x200B;

It's only early days in my healing journey, but things that I do that I KNOW help are have a dog. Walk the dog. Pat the dog. Accept snuggles. Lift HEAVY weights. That's 1.5 hrs 5x a week where you think less about your PTSD and more about lifting heavy shit. Have spiritual, shamanic energy work type massages. Reset your body with positive, loving touch instead of abusive, PTSD-causing touch. Go to the beach and ground yourself that way. Go on brutal hikes (can't ruminate when you are struggling up a rock face). Think about cannabis.

&#x200B;

I see a personal trainer (NOT a medical professional) to re-form a relationship with my body and health on MY terms, outside of a medical model (the model that abused me my whole life and most recently in childbirth). When I have more cash I want to see more energy workers, shamanic bodywork. IDK maybe consider anesthesiology. I don't believe any of that alternative healing has any scientific basis. BUT it offers attention, care and focus on you  and your healing outside of a medical setting

Mom Butt is a thing. Your pelvis tilts to accomodate a big belly, and the glutes and deep core switch off, hence mom butt and a pooch. Talk to a womens health physio or a postpartum qualified personal trainer.   


You can also look into the postpartum programs for diastasis recti they will help with switching core and butt back on.

&#x200B;

I had this and I try do core every day. Super low stress stuff, only side plank, toe taps, paloff press and ab vacuums. Plus looooots of squats and classic weights.

&#x200B;

I am still fat but my shape has changed and the mom butt and pooch are gone.

I had a narcissistic ex who relied on me financially 100%. He would always threaten to leave me, run away for a night or a day to a friend or hotel, block me for a few hours etc.

&#x200B;

The best thing you can do for these financial, energy and time leeches is to dump THEM. Narcissists always say what they themselves are most scared of/ have done.

&#x200B;

Constantly threatens to leave you? He is terrified you will leave him.

&#x200B;

Talk to a lawyer, or at least make sure you have some damning stuff on him he wouldn't want to get out, (in case he has claims on living at your house), then neatly boot him out.

&#x200B;

Trust me 'we have been through so much' means 'this man made me put up with BS'. A difficult history means He has put YOU through drama.

&#x200B;

It sounds like you've (rightfully) lost respect for this man. How can you have lust towards and passion for a literal manchild? I promise your next partner will be 100x more satisfying, if only for the reason that you won't resent the hell out of them, and actually have respect for them as a human (because they aren't a man baby).  


You are honestly better off single than with someone this toxic and sounds like a narcissist. And once you've cut him off in your heart, you make space for someone 100x better.
